{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528188196,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2371709002WOBnpc2SmpGZA6","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","messageID":"msg_1d237078a001cmZtktxmCmxElq","type":"tool","id":"call_8e567627792d4db2b11641ae","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaults | `specs/**` | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` |\n187: | SKILLED | `.skilled/**` | `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n188: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/**` | `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n189: | SKILLED | `.skilled/agents/**` | `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n190: | SKILLED | `.skilled/commands/**` | `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n191: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.cjs`, `.mjs` | `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n192: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md` |\n193: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md` |\n194: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md` |\n195: | SKILLED | `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.mts`, `.cts` | `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n196: | SKILLED | `.py` | `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n197: | SKILLED | `.sh`, `.bash` | `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n198: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md` |\n199: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md` |\n200: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md` |\n201: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md` |\n202: | SKILLED | `.json`, `.jsonc`, `.yaml`, `.yml` | `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n203: | sk-code-ci | `.github/workflows/**` | `../sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaults | `specs/**` | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` |\n187: | SKILLED | `.skilled/**` | `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n188: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/**` | `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n189: | SKILLED | `.skilled/agents/**` | `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n190: | SKILLED | `.skilled/commands/**` | `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n191: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.cjs`, `.mjs` | `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n192: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md` |\n193: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md` |\n194: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md` |\n195: | SKILLED | `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.mts`, `.cts` | `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n196: | SKILLED | `.py` | `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n197: | SKILLED | `.sh`, `.bash` | `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n198: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md` |\n199: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md` |\n200: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md` |\n201: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md` |\n202: | SKILLED | `.json`, `.jsonc`, `.yaml`, `.yml` | `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n203: | sk-code-ci | `.github/workflows/**` | `../sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528188169,"end":1794528188196}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794528192027,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","part":{"id":"prt_1d237261b001d904iPGXKhoH3B","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","messageID":"msg_1d237078a001cmZtktxmCmxElq","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"3efccfbec1dd7d4abe28b849d36761b3f9100432","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":312,"output":76,"reasoning":69,"cache":{"read":65024,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794528194461,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","part":{"id":"prt_1d2372f9d001ZrvLgB4BXOWOx3","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","messageID":"msg_1d2372626001qtJDYlpJJY37CK","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ca96101ed4760b65794d0d35fea1e87014ccb1b6"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528194951,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2372fd10018YE2X0G8mSCydr","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb3241ffeAG7LK08NNfAIgF","messageID":"msg_1d2372626001qtJDYlpJJY37CK","type":"tool","id":"call_ea5ccd0eb32340b6bb0c8b75","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528194513,"end":1794528194951}}}}
